====================================================
FACTORY METHOD DESIGN PATTERN (C++ INTERVIEW NOTES)
====================================================


====================================================
1. WHAT IS FACTORY METHOD?
====================================================

Factory Method is a creational design pattern that:

- Defines an interface for creating objects.
- Lets derived classes decide which concrete object to create.
- Separates object creation logic from client code.

CORE IDEA:

Client should not create concrete objects directly.

Instead:

Client → Factory → Product


The client only knows the interface, not the actual implementation.


====================================================
2. WHY DO WE NEED FACTORY METHOD?
====================================================

WITHOUT FACTORY METHOD:

Example:

class Client
{
    if(type == 1)
        obj = new TwoWheeler();

    else if(type == 2)
        obj = new FourWheeler();
};


PROBLEMS:

1. Tight coupling
-----------------
Client directly depends on concrete classes.

Client knows:
- TwoWheeler
- FourWheeler


2. Violates Open/Closed Principle
---------------------------------
Open for extension,
Closed for modification.


Example:

New class added:

class ThreeWheeler


Without Factory:

Need to modify Client:

if(type == 3)
    obj = new ThreeWheeler();


Every new class requires:
- Client modification
- Recompilation
- Testing again


3. Not scalable
---------------
Large systems may have hundreds of object types.

Object creation logic becomes complex.


====================================================
3. REAL WORLD EXAMPLE
====================================================

Vehicle Manufacturing System


User:

"I need a vehicle"


System internally decides:

- Bike
- Car
- Truck


User does not know:
- Which class is created
- How object is created


====================================================
4. FACTORY METHOD FLOW
====================================================


WITHOUT FACTORY:

Client
  |
  |
if-else
  |
  |
Concrete Object


Problem:
Client knows everything.


----------------------------------------------


WITH FACTORY METHOD:


Client

  |

Factory Interface

  |

Concrete Factory

  |

Concrete Product


====================================================
5. FACTORY METHOD STRUCTURE
====================================================


Components:

1. Product Interface

   Common interface for all objects.


2. Concrete Products

   Actual implementations.


3. Factory Interface

   Defines object creation method.


4. Concrete Factories

   Decide which object to create.


====================================================
6. FACTORY METHOD C++ IMPLEMENTATION
====================================================


#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

using namespace std;



====================================================
PRODUCT INTERFACE
====================================================


class Vehicle
{
public:

    virtual void printVehicle() = 0;

    virtual ~Vehicle() = default;
};



====================================================
CONCRETE PRODUCTS
====================================================


class TwoWheeler : public Vehicle
{
public:

    void printVehicle() override
    {
        cout<<"Two Wheeler"<<endl;
    }
};



class FourWheeler : public Vehicle
{
public:

    void printVehicle() override
    {
        cout<<"Four Wheeler"<<endl;
    }
};



====================================================
FACTORY INTERFACE
====================================================


class VehicleFactory
{
public:

    virtual unique_ptr<Vehicle> createVehicle() = 0;

    virtual ~VehicleFactory() = default;
};



====================================================
CONCRETE FACTORIES
====================================================


class TwoWheelerFactory : public VehicleFactory
{
public:

    unique_ptr<Vehicle> createVehicle() override
    {
        return make_unique<TwoWheeler>();
    }
};



class FourWheelerFactory : public VehicleFactory
{
public:

    unique_ptr<Vehicle> createVehicle() override
    {
        return make_unique<FourWheeler>();
    }
};



====================================================
CLIENT
====================================================


class Client
{

private:

    unique_ptr<Vehicle> vehicle;


public:

    Client(VehicleFactory& factory)
    {
        vehicle = factory.createVehicle();
    }


    void show()
    {
        vehicle->printVehicle();
    }

};



====================================================
MAIN
====================================================


int main()
{

    TwoWheelerFactory twoFactory;

    Client c1(twoFactory);

    c1.show();



    FourWheelerFactory fourFactory;

    Client c2(fourFactory);

    c2.show();


    return 0;
}


====================================================
7. HOW FACTORY METHOD SOLVES THE PROBLEM?
====================================================


Before:

Client creates objects directly.

Client
 |
 |
TwoWheeler
FourWheeler


Problem:
Tight coupling.



After:

Client only knows interface.

Client
 |
 |
VehicleFactory
 |
 |
Concrete Factory
 |
 |
Vehicle


Benefits:

- Client does not depend on concrete classes.
- New products can be added easily.
- Existing client code does not change.



Example:

Add:

class ThreeWheeler : public Vehicle


Create:

class ThreeWheelerFactory : public VehicleFactory


No client modification required.


====================================================
8. BENEFITS
====================================================


✔ Loose coupling

✔ Follows Open/Closed Principle

✔ Easy extension

✔ Better maintainability

✔ Centralized object creation

✔ Cleaner architecture



====================================================
9. FACTORY METHOD vs SIMPLE FACTORY
====================================================


SIMPLE FACTORY:

Example:

Factory::create(type)


Implementation:

if(type == CAR)
    return new Car();

else if(type == BIKE)
    return new Bike();



Characteristics:

- One factory class.
- Uses if/else or switch.
- Easy initially.
- Adding new types requires modifying factory.
- Not fully OCP compliant.



----------------------------------------------


FACTORY METHOD:


Characteristics:

- Uses inheritance.
- Each factory creates one type of object.
- Adding new products requires adding new factory.
- Existing code remains unchanged.
- Follows OCP better.



